window.CNPE_DRILL = [
{"id": "1.1#a-service-resolves-in-dns-but-every-connection-i", "sec": "1.1", "q": "A Service resolves in DNS but every connection is refused. Name the three things you check, in order.", "a": "<p>Selector against actual pod labels; pod readiness (an unready pod is not in the slice); the EndpointSlice itself. If the slice has addresses with <code>ready: false<\/code>, it is a probe problem, not a Service problem. If the slice is empty, it is a selector or a scheduling problem.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.1#why-does-a-networkpolicy-ipblock-naming-a-servic", "sec": "1.1", "q": "Why does a NetworkPolicy <code>ipBlock<\/code> naming a Service's ClusterIP never work?", "a": "<p>Because DNAT happens before policy evaluation: by the time the packet is checked, its destination is a pod IP. Policy is expressed in pod selectors and identities, not virtual IPs. For the API server specifically, Cilium's <code>toEntities: [kube-apiserver]<\/code> is the expressible form.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.1#your-backend-needs-the-real-client-ip-what-do-yo", "sec": "1.1", "q": "Your backend needs the real client IP. What do you change, and what breaks?", "a": "<p><code>externalTrafficPolicy: Local<\/code> on the Service. The trade: nodes with no backing pod stop serving that traffic, so you now depend on the load balancer's health checks to avoid them, and you lose the even spread that <code>Cluster<\/code> gave you.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.1#what-is-the-difference-between-a-headless-servic", "sec": "1.1", "q": "What is the difference between a headless Service and a ClusterIP Service with one endpoint?", "a": "<p>Headless has no VIP and no datapath translation: DNS returns pod IPs directly and the client chooses. A one-endpoint ClusterIP still goes through the VIP and its rules, so the client never learns the pod IP. StatefulSets need the former for stable per-pod names (<code>pod-0.svc…<\/code>).<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.1#team-a-can-curl-team-b-s-service-after-you-added", "sec": "1.1", "q": "Team-a can curl team-b's Service after you added an egress allow, but it still fails. What did you forget?", "a": "<p>Team-b's ingress side. Both ends must open; policies are additive allow-lists evaluated independently at each pod. Also check DNS: if egress is now restricted in team-a, resolving <code>web.team-b.svc<\/code> needs its own port-53 allow.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.1#an-httproute-applies-cleanly-but-nothing-routes-", "sec": "1.1", "q": "An HTTPRoute applies cleanly but nothing routes. Where do you look first?", "a": "<p><code>status.parents[].conditions<\/code> on the route: <em>Accepted<\/em> false usually means attachment was refused by the Gateway's <code>allowedRoutes<\/code>; <em>ResolvedRefs<\/em> false means a backend Service or a TLS secret is missing (or needs a ReferenceGrant across namespaces). On the Gateway, <em>Programmed<\/em> false means no data plane, which is the permanent state on this lab's main cluster.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.2#a-node-shows-25-cpu-usage-and-refuses-to-schedul", "sec": "1.2", "q": "A node shows 25% CPU usage and refuses to schedule a pod requesting 200m. Explain, in one sentence.", "a": "<p>The scheduler counts <em>requests<\/em> against <em>allocatable<\/em>, not usage: the node's requests are already at allocatable even though the processes are idle. Fix by right-sizing the existing requests (or adding capacity), not by adding CPU headroom that already exists.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.2#which-pod-does-the-kubelet-evict-first-under-mem", "sec": "1.2", "q": "Which pod does the kubelet evict first under memory pressure, and why?", "a": "<p>BestEffort, because it declared no memory request at all and therefore ranks worst. Then Burstable pods exceeding their requests, ordered by how far over they are. Guaranteed last. The kernel's <code>oom_score_adj<\/code> mirrors that ranking so a system-level OOM picks the same victim.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.2#your-service-has-p99-latency-spikes-every-few-se", "sec": "1.2", "q": "Your service has p99 latency spikes every few seconds; CPU usage sits at 60% of the limit. First metric you check?", "a": "<p><code>rate(container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total[5m])<\/code>. Average usage below the limit hides per-period throttling: the container burns its 100ms quota early and stalls until the next period. Average utilisation is the wrong lens for CPU limits.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.2#why-can-a-vpa-in-auto-mode-and-an-hpa-on-cpu-not", "sec": "1.2", "q": "Why can a VPA in <code>Auto<\/code> mode and an HPA on CPU not coexist on the same workload?", "a": "<p>They form a loop: the HPA scales replicas based on usage-against-requests while the VPA rewrites those same requests, so each one keeps invalidating the other's denominator. Standard resolution: HPA on CPU with VPA in <code>Off<\/code> (advisory), or HPA on a custom/external metric while VPA owns the resources.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.2#a-drain-hangs-forever-on-one-node-what-are-the-t", "sec": "1.2", "q": "A drain hangs forever on one node. What are the two usual causes?", "a": "<p>A PodDisruptionBudget that cannot be satisfied (often <code>minAvailable<\/code> equal to the replica count), or unmanaged pods: bare pods with no controller, which <code>kubectl drain<\/code> refuses to evict without <code>--force<\/code>. Both are voluntary-disruption mechanics; neither has anything to do with node health.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.3#a-pvc-has-been-pending-for-ten-minutes-and-descr", "sec": "1.3", "q": "A PVC has been Pending for ten minutes and <code>describe<\/code> shows no events at all. Bug or not?", "a": "<p>Not a bug if the class uses <code>WaitForFirstConsumer<\/code> and nothing mounts the claim yet: no scheduling decision exists, so there is nothing to provision and nothing to report. It becomes a finding the moment a pod references it and the claim <em>stays<\/em> Pending; then the events appear and name the real reason.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.3#a-pv-sits-in-released-and-your-new-identical-pvc", "sec": "1.3", "q": "A PV sits in Released and your new, identical PVC will not bind to it. Why, and what is the fix?", "a": "<p>Retain leaves <code>spec.claimRef<\/code> pointing at the deleted claim, and a PV with a claimRef is spoken for. Clear it (<code>kubectl patch pv &lt;name&gt; -p '{\"spec\":{\"claimRef\":null}}'<\/code>) and it returns to Available. Deciding whether the old data should be wiped first is the actual judgement call.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.3#you-delete-a-statefulset-and-its-pvcs-remain-acc", "sec": "1.3", "q": "You delete a StatefulSet and its PVCs remain. Accident or design?", "a": "<p>Design. The claims outlive the set so scaling back up reattaches the same data, and so an accidental delete does not destroy the database. Newer clusters can opt in to cleanup via <code>persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy<\/code> (<code>whenDeleted<\/code> / <code>whenScaled<\/code>).<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.3#two-pods-on-different-nodes-must-share-a-directo", "sec": "1.3", "q": "Two pods on different nodes must share a directory. What do you need, and what will this lab do?", "a": "<p>A class whose provisioner supports RWX (NFS, CephFS, a cloud file service). local-path cannot, so the claim pends and the events say so. The honest lab answer is \"not possible here\" plus the fix you would apply in a real cluster, which is also the right exam answer when the storage cannot do what the task implies.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.3#which-quota-lines-cap-tenant-storage-and-why-is-", "sec": "1.3", "q": "Which quota lines cap tenant storage, and why is per-class quota interesting?", "a": "<p><code>requests.storage<\/code> and <code>persistentvolumeclaims<\/code> cap the total and the count; <code>&lt;class&gt;.storageclass.storage.k8s.io/requests.storage<\/code> caps a specific class. The per-class form is how a platform team offers a fast expensive tier without every tenant defaulting to it: a pricing decision expressed as a Kubernetes object.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.4#a-tenant-applies-a-deployment-it-is-accepted-and", "sec": "1.4", "q": "A tenant applies a Deployment. It is accepted, and no pods appear. Where is the error?", "a": "<p>One level down, on the ReplicaSet: quota and PSS are pod-level admission decisions, and the Deployment controller only records the failure in the RS's events and conditions. <code>kubectl -n &lt;ns&gt; describe rs -l app=&lt;x&gt;<\/code> or namespace events. This indirection is a stock exam scenario.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.4#why-does-adding-a-limitrange-sometimes-make-prev", "sec": "1.4", "q": "Why does adding a LimitRange sometimes make previously-working pods fail?", "a": "<p>Because it enforces <code>min<\/code>/<code>max<\/code> as well as injecting defaults. Existing pods keep running (admission is not retroactive), but the next pod whose explicit requests fall outside the bounds is rejected, and the message comes from the LimitRange, not the quota.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.4#quota-says-requests-cpu-2-a-tenant-asks-why-they", "sec": "1.4", "q": "Quota says <code>requests.cpu: \"2\"<\/code>. A tenant asks why they cannot run 40 pods when the LimitRange injects 50m each.", "a": "<p>Because the other quota lines bind first: <code>limits.cpu: \"4\"<\/code> against a 200m default limit allows 20 pods, and <code>pods: \"20\"<\/code> caps it at the same number. Whichever binds first is the one named in the event. The lesson: quota is a conjunction, and the tightest line wins.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.4#name-the-isolation-ladder-and-one-cost-of-each-r", "sec": "1.4", "q": "Name the isolation ladder and one cost of each rung.", "a": "<p>Namespace + quota + policy (cheap, shared kernel and shared API server). Dedicated node pools via taints (no shared kernel; stranded capacity per pool). Virtual clusters (own API server and CRDs; more moving parts to run). Separate clusters (strongest; multiplied operational and idle cost). Pick the thinnest rung that satisfies the actual threat model.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.4#why-is-services-loadbalancers-1-in-a-tenant-quot", "sec": "1.4", "q": "Why is <code>services.loadbalancers: \"1\"<\/code> in a tenant quota?", "a": "<p>Because a LoadBalancer bills outside the cluster whether or not traffic flows. Quota here is a spend control, not a fairness control: the same reasoning that puts per-StorageClass storage quota in a tenant template.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.5#a-namespace-runs-almost-no-traffic-and-still-top", "sec": "1.5", "q": "A namespace runs almost no traffic and still tops the cost report. Give the two most likely explanations.", "a": "<p>Oversized requests (billed on reservation, not usage), or expensive attached objects: LoadBalancers, provisioned volumes, retained snapshots. Check efficiency first: very low efficiency points at requests; high efficiency with high cost points at the objects.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.5#why-is-container-in-that-promql-query-not-option", "sec": "1.5", "q": "Why is <code>container!=\"\"<\/code> in that PromQL query not optional?", "a": "<p>cadvisor emits per-container series plus pod-level and node-level rollups with an empty <code>container<\/code> label, and often the pause container as <code>container=\"POD\"<\/code>. Summing everything counts the same CPU two or three times, so every aggregate over cadvisor series needs <code>{container!=\"\",container!=\"POD\"}<\/code> or an equivalent.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.5#should-idle-cost-be-spread-across-tenants-or-sho", "sec": "1.5", "q": "Should idle cost be spread across tenants or shown separately? Argue both.", "a": "<p>Spread: tenants see the true cost of the cluster they share, which discourages hoarding. Separate: the platform team owns bin-packing and node choice, so making idle visible as <em>their<\/em> number is what drives consolidation. Mature setups show both: tenant cost at request-level, plus a platform-owned idle line.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.5#you-cut-a-deployment-s-cpu-request-from-500m-to-", "sec": "1.5", "q": "You cut a deployment's CPU request from 500m to 50m and latency degrades. What happened?", "a": "<p>Requests set the cgroup CPU weight, so under contention the container now gets a much smaller share; and if a limit exists near the old request, throttling is likelier. Right-sizing means matching observed usage with headroom for peaks, not matching the average. Confirm with throttling metrics, not with the cost graph.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.5#give-the-four-step-right-sizing-loop-in-one-brea", "sec": "1.5", "q": "Give the four-step right-sizing loop in one breath.", "a": "<p>Measure usage → compare with requests → adjust requests → confirm no degradation (no throttling, no OOM, no Pending) and re-measure efficiency. The fourth step is the one that makes it engineering rather than cost-cutting.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.1#state-the-four-opengitops-principles-without-loo", "sec": "2.1", "q": "State the four OpenGitOps principles without looking.", "a": "<p>Declarative; versioned and immutable; pulled automatically by agents; continuously reconciled. If you can only remember three, the one people drop is \"versioned and immutable\", which is also the one that carries the audit and rollback story.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.1#why-are-environment-branches-discouraged", "sec": "2.1", "q": "Why are environment branches discouraged?", "a": "<p>Long-lived branches accumulate divergent changes, so promotion becomes a merge with conflicts and cherry-picks rather than a reviewable diff, and the environments drift structurally rather than only in the values you meant to differ. Directories keep the difference explicit and diffable.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.1#a-colleague-fixes-production-with-kubectl-edit-d", "sec": "2.1", "q": "A colleague fixes production with <code>kubectl edit<\/code> during an incident. What happens next, and what should they have done?", "a": "<p>Self-heal reverts it at the next reconcile (or it is reported as drift). The sanctioned move is to suspend/disable reconciliation for that app, apply the emergency change, then land the same change in git and resume, so the fix survives and the history records it.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.1#what-does-prune-actually-change-and-what-is-the-", "sec": "2.1", "q": "What does prune actually change, and what is the failure mode in each setting?", "a": "<p>Prune deletes live resources whose manifests disappeared from git. Off: renamed or removed resources linger forever as invisible cruft. On: deleting a file deletes the running thing, so a careless refactor is a production outage. Neither is safe by default; the safety comes from knowing which one you have.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.1#where-do-secrets-live-in-a-gitops-repo", "sec": "2.1", "q": "Where do secrets live in a GitOps repo?", "a": "<p>Encrypted (Sealed Secrets or SOPS) or by reference (External Secrets pointing at a real store). Never plain, never base64-only. The distinction to state: Sealed Secrets/SOPS keep ciphertext in git; ESO keeps only a pointer and leaves truth in the external store.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.2#an-app-is-synced-degraded-what-are-you-allowed-t", "sec": "2.2", "q": "An app is Synced/Degraded. What are you allowed to conclude, and what is the fix?", "a": "<p>Live matches git, so Argo CD has done its job; the desired state itself is broken (bad image, impossible resources, missing config key). The fix is a commit. Re-syncing changes nothing because the diff is already empty.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.2#a-sync-fails-with-field-is-immutable-two-ways-fo", "sec": "2.2", "q": "A sync fails with \"field is immutable\". Two ways forward?", "a": "<p>Set <code>Replace=true<\/code> (per app or per resource) so the sync uses <code>kubectl replace<\/code> instead of apply, or delete the resource and let Argo recreate it; <code>Force=true<\/code> combines the two. Choose deliberately: replace is not a free pass, since a Service manifest that omits <code>clusterIP<\/code> is still rejected as immutable, and a shrinking PVC is not going to be saved by either option.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.2#what-is-an-appproject-for-in-one-sentence-and-na", "sec": "2.2", "q": "What is an AppProject for, in one sentence, and name two things it constrains?", "a": "<p>It is the guardrail around a set of Applications: permitted source repos and permitted destinations (cluster + namespace), plus allowed/denied resource kinds, per-project RBAC roles and sync windows. It is the answer to \"let this team self-serve deployments without letting them deploy anything anywhere\".<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.2#you-are-handed-an-applicationset-with-a-git-dire", "sec": "2.2", "q": "You are handed an ApplicationSet with a git directory generator over <code>apps/*/overlays/*<\/code>. How many Applications will exist?", "a": "<p>One per matching directory in the repo at the current revision, so count the directories, then check the template for a name expression that could collide (two identical names silently overwrite). Predicting the exact set from the generator block is the testable skill; <code>kubectl -n argocd get applications<\/code> then confirms it.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.2#an-app-shows-outofsync-forever-on-a-field-no-hum", "sec": "2.2", "q": "An app shows OutOfSync forever on a field no human edits. What is happening and what fixes it?", "a": "<p>Something in-cluster mutates the resource after apply: a mutating webhook, an HPA writing replicas, a defaulting controller. Fix with <code>ignoreDifferences<\/code> for that path (or stop managing the field). This is a configuration decision, not a bug to chase.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.2#you-changed-the-repo-but-argo-insists-nothing-ch", "sec": "2.2", "q": "You changed the repo but Argo insists nothing changed. Sequence of moves?", "a": "<p><code>argocd app get --refresh<\/code> first (recompare), then <code>--hard-refresh<\/code> (drop the manifest cache), then check the app is on the branch you pushed to (<code>targetRevision<\/code>), then check repo-server logs for auth or render errors. Cheapest first, always.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.3#flux-has-no-selfheal-setting-why-is-drift-still-", "sec": "2.3", "q": "Flux has no <code>selfHeal<\/code> setting. Why is drift still corrected?", "a": "<p>Because the Kustomization re-applies its rendered manifests every <code>interval<\/code>; correcting drift is a side effect of applying, not a separate feature. The corollary: your drift window is bounded by the interval, and shortening it costs API server load.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.3#a-kustomization-is-ready-false-name-the-three-la", "sec": "2.3", "q": "A Kustomization is Ready False. Name the three layers where it could have failed.", "a": "<p>Source (the GitRepository is not Ready: auth, ref, or network), build (kustomize build failed, bad path or invalid YAML), apply/health (the API server rejected something, or health checks timed out). The conditions and <code>flux logs<\/code> name the layer; do not skip straight to the manifests.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.3#how-do-you-order-install-crds-then-the-operator-", "sec": "2.3", "q": "How do you order \"install CRDs, then the operator, then the tenant CRs\" in Flux?", "a": "<p>Three Kustomizations with <code>dependsOn<\/code>, each with <code>wait: true<\/code> (or explicit <code>healthChecks<\/code>) so a dependency counts as satisfied only when its objects are actually healthy. Without wait/healthChecks, <code>dependsOn<\/code> only orders the <em>apply<\/em>, not the readiness.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.3#what-is-postbuild-substitute-for-and-what-is-its", "sec": "2.3", "q": "What is <code>postBuild.substitute<\/code> for, and what is its Argo CD analogue?", "a": "<p>Injecting per-cluster or per-environment values into manifests after kustomize build, sourced inline or from ConfigMaps/Secrets. Argo's analogues are kustomize overlays, Helm values per Application, or ApplicationSet template parameters; Flux just lets you do it without another overlay directory.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.3#how-does-flux-stop-one-tenant-s-kustomization-fr", "sec": "2.3", "q": "How does Flux stop one tenant's Kustomization from deploying cluster-admin-level resources?", "a": "<p><code>spec.serviceAccountName<\/code>: the controller impersonates that ServiceAccount when applying, so the tenant's manifests can never exceed the tenant's RBAC. Combined with disallowing cross-namespace source references, that is Flux's multi-tenancy story: the structural equivalent of Argo's AppProject.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.4#two-tasks-in-a-pipeline-run-at-the-same-time-and", "sec": "2.4", "q": "Two tasks in a pipeline run at the same time and you did not expect it. Why?", "a": "<p>No <code>runAfter<\/code> and no result dependency between them, so Tekton runs them concurrently by design. Order comes from explicit <code>runAfter<\/code> or from consuming a result; nothing else implies sequence.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.4#how-do-files-get-from-the-clone-task-to-the-buil", "sec": "2.4", "q": "How do files get from the clone task to the build task, and what happens if you bind the workspace to an emptyDir?", "a": "<p>Through a workspace backed by a PVC or <code>volumeClaimTemplate<\/code>, mounted by both tasks. An emptyDir is per-pod, so each task gets an empty one and the build finds nothing: a classic \"why is my workspace empty\" bug.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.4#where-do-you-put-a-step-that-must-run-whether-th", "sec": "2.4", "q": "Where do you put a step that must run whether the pipeline passed or failed?", "a": "<p><code>spec.finally<\/code>. It runs after all other tasks regardless of outcome, and it can inspect <code>$(tasks.status)<\/code> to branch; cleanup, notifications, and teardown belong there rather than as a last <code>runAfter<\/code> task that never executes on failure.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.4#why-does-the-pipeline-push-to-kind-registry-5000", "sec": "2.4", "q": "Why does the pipeline push to <code>kind-registry:5000<\/code> and not <code>localhost:5001<\/code>?", "a": "<p>Because it runs in a pod, where <code>localhost<\/code> is the pod's own network namespace. The registry has an in-cluster DNS name wired into CoreDNS and containerd; the host reaches the same store through a published port. Same content, two names, and the digest is identical from both.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.4#your-eventlistener-is-ready-and-a-push-produces-", "sec": "2.4", "q": "Your EventListener is Ready and a push produces nothing. Diagnostic ladder?", "a": "<p>Did the webhook deliver (Gitea's webhook delivery log)? Did the listener receive it (its pod logs)? Did an interceptor filter it out (event type, secret mismatch, CEL expression)? Did the binding extract fields the template expects (a missing param yields an invalid PipelineRun)? Each rung produces a different error; check them in order.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.5#you-set-setweight-10-with-3-replicas-and-no-traf", "sec": "2.5", "q": "You set <code>setWeight: 10<\/code> with 3 replicas and no traffic provider. What actually happens?", "a": "<p>Rollouts rounds to whole pods: it runs one canary pod alongside the stable set, so the real split is nearer 25–33%, not 10%. Exact weights require a traffic provider (Istio, Gateway API, NGINX) or more replicas.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.5#a-canary-aborted-and-the-analysisrun-shows-measu", "sec": "2.5", "q": "A canary aborted and the AnalysisRun shows measurements in phase Error. What do you check?", "a": "<p>The metric query and its provider: wrong series name, no data yet, unreachable Prometheus, or a selector that matches nothing. Errors count toward <code>consecutiveErrorLimit<\/code> (default 4), so a broken query aborts releases exactly like a bad release does; check the measurement message before touching the application.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.5#blue-green-with-autopromotionenabled-false-which", "sec": "2.5", "q": "Blue-green with <code>autoPromotionEnabled: false<\/code>: which Service points where, before and after promote?", "a": "<p>Before: active selects the old ReplicaSet's <code>rollouts-pod-template-hash<\/code>, preview selects the new one. After: both select the new hash, and the old ReplicaSet lingers for <code>scaleDownDelaySeconds<\/code> so rollback is a selector flip away.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.5#argo-cd-manages-your-rollout-you-abort-a-bad-can", "sec": "2.5", "q": "Argo CD manages your Rollout. You abort a bad canary. What happens next, and what should you do?", "a": "<p>The Rollout spec in git still names the bad image, so the next sync re-applies it and the rollout starts again. Revert the commit (or pin the tag back): abort is an operational stop, git is the desired state. Same lesson as any manual fix under GitOps.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.5#name-two-things-that-make-progressive-delivery-u", "sec": "2.5", "q": "Name two things that make progressive delivery unsafe regardless of tooling.", "a": "<p>Database or API changes that both versions cannot tolerate simultaneously (fix with expand-and-contract migrations and backwards-compatible contracts), and metrics too sparse or too coarse to detect harm within the canary window. A third: analysis that measures the stable version by accident.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.6#the-app-is-synced-and-healthy-but-the-feature-we", "sec": "2.6", "q": "\"The app is Synced and Healthy but the feature we merged an hour ago is missing.\"", "a": "<p>Bucket 4, stale. Check for suspension, then the last sync timestamp and the resolved revision against your commit SHA, then whether the app tracks the branch you pushed to. Refresh/reconcile before anything else.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.6#sync-fails-service-demo-is-invalid-spec-clusteri", "sec": "2.6", "q": "\"Sync fails: <code>Service \"demo\" is invalid: spec.clusterIP: Invalid value: field is immutable<\/code>.\"", "a": "<p>Bucket 2: the cluster refused the manifest. The text came from the API server, relayed by the sync operation. Fix the manifest, or force replace semantics if the field genuinely must change (accepting the churn that implies).<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.6#argocd-repo-server-logs-show-authentication-requ", "sec": "2.6", "q": "\"argocd-repo-server logs show <code>authentication required<\/code>; the workload is untouched and running fine.\"", "a": "<p>Bucket 3: access. No pod of yours is implicated; the controller's identity is. Repo credentials, token scope, or a rotated secret. The workload keeps running because reconciliation is what broke, not the deployment.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.6#deployment-exists-no-pods-no-error-on-the-deploy", "sec": "2.6", "q": "\"Deployment exists, no pods, no error on the Deployment.\"", "a": "<p>Look one level down at the ReplicaSet: quota, PSS, or an admission webhook rejected the pod template. Same indirection as sections 1.4 and 5.3. Not strictly a delivery bug: the delivery worked, admission refused.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.6#i-keep-deleting-this-configmap-and-it-keeps-comi", "sec": "2.6", "q": "\"I keep deleting this ConfigMap and it keeps coming back.\"", "a": "<p>Something reconciles it: self-heal, a Flux Kustomization at interval, or an operator that owns it. Prove which with <code>ownerReferences<\/code>, the <code>app.kubernetes.io/instance<\/code> label, or <code>flux trace<\/code>. Then change the source of truth instead of the live object, or suspend first if you need a temporary window.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.1#define-a-platform-in-the-paper-s-own-terms", "sec": "3.1", "q": "Define a platform in the paper's own terms.", "a": "<p>An integrated collection of capabilities, defined and presented according to the needs of its users. The emphasis on <em>users<\/em> and on <em>presentation<\/em> (interfaces) is what distinguishes a platform from a pile of infrastructure.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.1#what-is-the-thinnest-viable-platform-and-what-do", "sec": "3.1", "q": "What is the Thinnest Viable Platform, and what does it imply about build-vs-adopt?", "a": "<p>The smallest layer that provides consistency and accelerates delivery. It implies adopting existing capabilities wherever they exist and spending your effort on interfaces, integration and golden paths: the parts nobody can buy for your organisation.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.1#a-team-can-request-a-namespace-through-a-form-an", "sec": "3.1", "q": "A team can request a namespace through a form, and someone approves it within a day. Is that self-service?", "a": "<p>No. The paper's definition requires fulfillment without a human in the loop. A form plus an approver is an interface over a ticket queue: operational maturity, not scalable. The fix is automated fulfillment with policy encoded in the API, and approval only where regulation demands it.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.1#name-the-dora-four-and-one-platform-specific-mea", "sec": "3.1", "q": "Name the DORA four and one platform-specific measure the paper adds.", "a": "<p>Deployment frequency, lead time for changes, time to restore service, change failure rate. The paper adds measures like latency from request to fulfillment of a capability, time to first contribution, and adoption, all platform-specific because they measure the platform, not the applications on it.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.1#which-platform-attribute-does-a-mandatory-non-co", "sec": "3.1", "q": "Which platform attribute does a mandatory, non-composable platform violate, and why does it matter practically?", "a": "<p>\"Optional and composable\". Practically: mandated platforms hide their own failures, because teams route around them quietly instead of filing the feedback that would improve them. Optionality is a feedback mechanism, not generosity.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.2#a-user-says-their-field-does-not-work-kubectl-ge", "sec": "3.2", "q": "A user says their field \"does not work\". <code>kubectl get -o yaml<\/code> shows it missing entirely, and no error was printed. What happened?", "a": "<p>Pruning: the field is not in the schema, so the API server dropped it on write. Either add it to the schema or, rarely, mark that subtree with <code>x-kubernetes-preserve-unknown-fields<\/code>. The user experience argument is for adding it properly.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.2#your-controller-s-status-writes-vanish-one-likel", "sec": "3.2", "q": "Your controller's status writes vanish. One likely cause?", "a": "<p>The status subresource is enabled and the writer is going through the main endpoint, which ignores the <code>status<\/code> stanza. Use <code>UpdateStatus()<\/code> (or <code>kubectl patch --subresource=status<\/code>). The mirror-image bug also exists: with no subresource at all, a spec update from anyone overwrites the status your controller just wrote.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.2#you-must-forbid-changing-spec-tier-after-creatio", "sec": "3.2", "q": "You must forbid changing <code>spec.tier<\/code> after creation. How, without a webhook?", "a": "<p>A CEL validation on the field: <code>rule: \"self == oldSelf\"<\/code> with a message like \"tier is immutable\". <code>x-kubernetes-validations<\/code> is evaluated by the API server and can compare against the old object, which is exactly the immutability case.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.2#you-want-to-serve-v1alpha1-and-v1beta1-with-diff", "sec": "3.2", "q": "You want to serve <code>v1alpha1<\/code> and <code>v1beta1<\/code> with different shapes. What do you need?", "a": "<p>Both versions <code>served<\/code>, exactly one marked <code>storage<\/code>, and a conversion strategy: <code>None<\/code> only if the shapes are compatible, otherwise a conversion webhook. Everything read from etcd is stored in the storage version and converted on the way out.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.2#what-does-a-crd-give-you-for-free-and-what-does-", "sec": "3.2", "q": "What does a CRD give you for free, and what does it definitively not give you?", "a": "<p>Free: persistence, validation, defaulting, RBAC integration, watch/informers, <code>kubectl<\/code> support and explain docs, quota counting, policy-engine visibility. Not free: any behaviour whatsoever. Nothing happens until a controller acts on the object.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.3#you-patch-a-cr-and-its-status-still-reports-the-", "sec": "3.3", "q": "You patch a CR and its status still reports the old problem. What do you check before believing it?", "a": "<p><code>status.observedGeneration<\/code> against <code>metadata.generation<\/code>. If they differ, the controller has not processed your edit yet and the status describes the previous spec. If they match and the status is still wrong, now it is a real finding.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.3#a-namespace-has-been-terminating-for-twenty-minu", "sec": "3.3", "q": "A namespace has been Terminating for twenty minutes. Method?", "a": "<p>Find what is left (<code>kubectl api-resources --verbs=list --namespaced -o name | xargs -n1 kubectl get -n &lt;ns&gt;<\/code>), find the finalizer on it, and find whether the owning controller is running. The fix is usually reviving the controller; stripping the finalizer is a last resort that leaks whatever it was cleaning up.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.3#why-is-deleting-an-operator-managed-pod-not-a-wa", "sec": "3.3", "q": "Why is deleting an operator-managed pod not a way to \"reset\" anything?", "a": "<p>Because reconciliation is level-based: the controller observes that a pod is missing and creates one, with no memory of the event. If you need a different outcome, change the spec (or the underlying cause); the loop will faithfully restore whatever the spec says forever.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.3#an-operator-s-crs-apply-fine-and-nothing-happens", "sec": "3.3", "q": "An operator's CRs apply fine and nothing happens at all. Two checks?", "a": "<p>Is the controller running (and did it win leader election)? And does its RBAC cover your namespace and your kind? A Forbidden in the controller's logs is the classic silent failure. Only after both: is the CR shaped in a way the controller ignores (a selector, a class field, a paused annotation)?<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.3#name-the-five-ways-an-operator-integrates-with-t", "sec": "3.3", "q": "Name the five ways an operator integrates with the rest of a platform.", "a": "<p>Its CRs are delivered by GitOps; its metrics are scraped and alerted on; its connection Secrets are consumed by apps (or synced by ESO); its CRs are subject to policy at admission; and its API is fronted by a thinner developer-facing abstraction (Crossplane/kro/portal template). That list is the \"integration\" half of the competency.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.4#which-template-type-creates-kubernetes-objects-a", "sec": "3.4", "q": "Which template type creates Kubernetes objects, and what is its idempotency story?", "a": "<p><code>resource<\/code>. With <code>action: create<\/code> a re-run fails on AlreadyExists; with <code>action: apply<\/code> it converges. Workflows do not reconcile, so if the same request may be submitted twice, you design for it: apply semantics, or a guard step.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.4#a-workflow-node-says-forbidden-where-do-you-look", "sec": "3.4", "q": "A workflow node says <code>forbidden<\/code>. Where do you look, and what is the classic first offender?", "a": "<p>The workflow's ServiceAccount and its bindings, not the workflow spec. The classic first offender in Argo is missing <code>create,patch<\/code> on <code>workflowtaskresults.argoproj.io<\/code>, the executor's own reporting channel, which fails before your intended resource permission is even exercised.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.4#when-is-a-workflow-the-wrong-tool-and-what-repla", "sec": "3.4", "q": "When is a workflow the wrong tool, and what replaces it?", "a": "<p>When the desired state must stay true indefinitely; a workflow runs once and forgets. Use a controller/operator, or a declarative composition engine (Crossplane, kro) whose CR keeps reconciling. Workflows are for sequences with an end.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.4#two-dag-tasks-with-no-dependencies-what-happens-", "sec": "3.4", "q": "Two DAG tasks with no <code>dependencies<\/code>: what happens, and how do you gate one on a condition?", "a": "<p>They run in parallel. Gate with <code>dependencies<\/code> for ordering and <code>when<\/code> for conditions (evaluated against parameters or a previous task's result). A validation node that everything depends on is the standard \"fail before side effects\" shape.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.4#what-turns-a-workflow-into-a-self-service-produc", "sec": "3.4", "q": "What turns a Workflow into a self-service product?", "a": "<p>Promotion to a WorkflowTemplate with typed, documented, defaulted parameters, a submit surface (UI, CLI, portal, or a Backstage action), input validation before side effects, and an auditable record of who requested what. Same content, packaged as an interface.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.5#name-the-three-objects-and-who-owns-each", "sec": "3.5", "q": "Name the three objects and who owns each.", "a": "<p>XRD (platform team: the promised API and its schema), Composition (platform team: the recipe, changeable without touching the API), XR (developer: an instance). The generated CRD is Crossplane's doing, not yours, which is the whole labour saving.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.5#an-xr-is-stuck-not-ready-and-the-message-is-unhe", "sec": "3.5", "q": "An XR is stuck not-Ready and the message is unhelpful. Diagnostic order?", "a": "<p>Packages first (<code>providers<\/code>, <code>functions<\/code> Installed + Healthy), then <code>crossplane resource trace<\/code>, then the composed resources' own conditions, then the underlying API error inside them. Bottom-up, because that is the direction failure propagates.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.5#why-can-a-namespaced-xr-not-compose-a-cluster-sc", "sec": "3.5", "q": "Why can a namespaced XR not compose a cluster-scoped resource directly?", "a": "<p>Ownership rules: a namespaced object cannot own a cluster-scoped one, and Crossplane relies on ownerReferences for lifecycle. The workaround the lab uses is a namespaced <code>Object<\/code> (provider-kubernetes) that <em>contains<\/em> the cluster-scoped manifest, authenticated with a <code>ClusterProviderConfig<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.5#you-edit-a-composition-what-happens-to-the-twelv", "sec": "3.5", "q": "You edit a Composition. What happens to the twelve XRs already using it?", "a": "<p>They all reconcile to the new recipe, without anyone touching them: powerful and blast-radius-shaped. If that matters, pin instances to a composition revision or version the composition and migrate deliberately.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.5#when-would-you-choose-kro-or-a-plain-operator-ov", "sec": "3.5", "q": "When would you choose kro or a plain operator over Crossplane?", "a": "<p>kro when you want the same declarative expansion with far less machinery and can accept a young API (3.6). A plain operator when the value is domain behaviour over time (failover, backup, upgrade orchestration) rather than composing existing resources. Crossplane's sweet spot is composing many things, including external cloud APIs, behind one abstraction.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.6#how-does-kro-know-to-create-the-namespace-before", "sec": "3.6", "q": "How does kro know to create the namespace before the quota?", "a": "<p>From the reference: the quota template uses <code>${ns.metadata.name}<\/code>, so kro builds a dependency graph from the CEL references and orders accordingly. You never write <code>dependsOn<\/code>, which also means a refactor that removes a reference can silently reorder your graph.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.6#a-team-asks-for-a-portal-so-developers-can-self-", "sec": "3.6", "q": "A team asks for \"a portal so developers can self-serve\". What do you ask before agreeing?", "a": "<p>What happens after the form is submitted. If fulfillment is still manual, the portal is a prettier ticket queue; the automation (templates → git → GitOps → controllers) is the actual work, and the portal is the last 10%. That is the white paper's interface-versus-capability distinction.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.6#nightly-image-rebuild-and-rescan-which-engine-an", "sec": "3.6", "q": "Nightly image rebuild-and-rescan: which engine, and why not the others?", "a": "<p>A CronWorkflow: scheduled, run-to-completion, DAG with retries and notifications. Not an operator (nothing to reconcile forever), not Tekton (no git event trigger, though it could run the pipeline), not Crossplane (no resource graph to maintain).<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.6#give-the-three-questions-that-decide-the-engine", "sec": "3.6", "q": "Give the three questions that decide the engine.", "a": "<p>Does state need reconciling forever or producing once? What is the trigger: API object, schedule, git event, human? Who is the audience: YAML author, form filler, or another system? Answer those three and the table picks itself.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.6#what-does-the-scm-provider-generator-actually-wa", "sec": "3.6", "q": "What does the SCM-provider generator actually watch, and how would you debug a repo that never appears?", "a": "<p>It queries the git host's API for repos in an org matching filters (name pattern, topic, path existence), then templates one Application per match. Debug by reading the generator block (org, filters, credentials Secret), then checking the ApplicationSet's status/conditions and controller logs for API errors, usually a token scope or a filter that does not match.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.1#your-servicemonitor-is-correct-and-no-target-app", "sec": "4.1", "q": "Your ServiceMonitor is correct and no target appears. List the checks in order.", "a": "<p>1) Prometheus's <code>serviceMonitorSelector<\/code> and <code>serviceMonitorNamespaceSelector<\/code>: does it even consider your monitor? 2) Does the monitor's selector match the Service's labels? 3) Is <code>endpoints[].port<\/code> the port <em>name<\/em>, and does the Service name it? 4) Does the Service have ready endpoints? 5) RBAC and NetworkPolicy between monitoring and the target namespace.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.1#why-is-sum-rate-x-5m-right-and-rate-sum-x-5m-wro", "sec": "4.1", "q": "Why is <code>sum(rate(x[5m]))<\/code> right and <code>rate(sum(x)[5m])<\/code> wrong?", "a": "<p>Counters reset per series; <code>rate<\/code> knows how to handle resets within a single series. Summing first merges series (and their resets) into a meaningless line, and the syntax is invalid anyway without a subquery. Rate first, aggregate second, always.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.1#how-do-you-alert-on-the-metric-disappeared-entir", "sec": "4.1", "q": "How do you alert on \"the metric disappeared entirely\"?", "a": "<p><code>absent(up{job=\"x\"} == 1)<\/code> or <code>absent(metric)<\/code>: an ordinary comparison returns no series when there is nothing to compare, so it can never fire. <code>absent()<\/code> exists precisely to turn \"no data\" into a value of 1.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.1#a-p95-query-returns-nan-two-plausible-reasons", "sec": "4.1", "q": "A p95 query returns NaN. Two plausible reasons?", "a": "<p>No observations in the window (nothing has been recorded yet, so all buckets are empty), or you aggregated away the <code>le<\/code> label that <code>histogram_quantile<\/code> needs. The canonical form keeps it: <code>histogram_quantile(0.95, sum by (le) (rate(x_bucket[5m])))<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.1#an-exporter-added-a-label-with-one-value-per-req", "sec": "4.1", "q": "An exporter added a label with one value per request. What happens, and what is the fix?", "a": "<p>Cardinality explosion: a new series per unique value, memory and TSDB growth, slow queries. Fix at ingestion with a <code>metricRelabeling<\/code> that drops the label or the metric, and upstream by removing it from the exporter. High-cardinality labels (user IDs, URLs with IDs, trace IDs) belong in logs and traces, not metric labels.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.2#an-alert-shows-firing-in-prometheus-and-nothing-", "sec": "4.2", "q": "An alert shows Firing in Prometheus and nothing arrived. Three candidate causes?", "a": "<p>A silence matches it; an inhibition rule suppresses it; or routing sent it to a receiver that is failing (check Alertmanager's own logs and its <code>alertmanager_notifications_failed_total<\/code>). A fourth, if it only just fired: <code>group_wait<\/code> has not elapsed.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.2#your-rule-applies-cleanly-and-never-evaluates-wh", "sec": "4.2", "q": "Your rule applies cleanly and never evaluates. What did you not check?", "a": "<p>The Prometheus object's <code>ruleSelector<\/code> (and its namespace selector). Unmatched rules are ignored silently, the same failure shape as ServiceMonitors, with the same first command: read the selector.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.2#silence-versus-inhibition-when-do-you-use-each", "sec": "4.2", "q": "Silence versus inhibition: when do you use each?", "a": "<p>Silence: temporary, human-initiated, targeted at a known maintenance or a known-noisy alert, with an expiry and a comment. Inhibition: a standing rule expressing a relationship: when the cause alert fires, suppress its downstream symptoms. Silences are operations; inhibitions are design.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.2#how-long-roughly-from-condition-becomes-true-to-", "sec": "4.2", "q": "How long, roughly, from \"condition becomes true\" to \"notification sent\" on a stock stack?", "a": "<p>Up to one evaluation interval (30s) to notice, plus <code>for<\/code> (say 1–5m), plus <code>group_wait<\/code> (30s). So a couple of minutes minimum, which is why you wait before declaring an alerting pipeline broken, and why <code>for: 15m<\/code> on a page-worthy symptom is usually too slow.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.2#what-is-the-watchdog-alert-for", "sec": "4.2", "q": "What is the Watchdog alert for?", "a": "<p>It fires permanently as a dead-man's switch: an external system expects to keep receiving it and alerts when it stops, catching the failure mode where Prometheus or Alertmanager itself dies and therefore cannot alert you about anything. Self-monitoring has to come from outside.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.3#how-does-a-dashboard-get-into-grafana-in-a-gitop", "sec": "4.3", "q": "How does a dashboard get into Grafana in a GitOps platform, and why not click it?", "a": "<p>JSON in a ConfigMap labelled <code>grafana_dashboard: \"1\"<\/code>, delivered from git; the sidecar loads it. A clicked dashboard lives only in Grafana's database: unreviewable, unversioned, and gone with the pod unless persistence is configured.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.3#explain-red-and-use-and-which-you-would-use-for-", "sec": "4.3", "q": "Explain RED and USE, and which you would use for a queue worker.", "a": "<p>RED = Rate, Errors, Duration, for request-driven services. USE = Utilization, Saturation, Errors, for resources. A queue worker is best served by both plus queue depth: USE for the worker pool, RED-ish for message processing, and saturation is really \"is the backlog growing\".<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.3#loki-returns-nothing-for-namespace-team-a-two-ho", "sec": "4.3", "q": "Loki returns nothing for <code>{namespace=\"team-a\"}<\/code>. Two hops to check, in order.", "a": "<p>The shipper first (Alloy running, not erroring on push, actually tailing that namespace), then Loki (ingester healthy, retention not eating the window, right tenant/org headers if multi-tenancy is on). A healthy Loki with no shipper looks fine and holds nothing, which is why <code>make validate<\/code> demands streams, not readiness.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.3#why-is-a-per-request-id-label-a-bad-idea-in-loki", "sec": "4.3", "q": "Why is a per-request-ID label a bad idea in Loki?", "a": "<p>Labels define streams, and Loki's index is per stream. A unique label value per request creates a stream per request: index explosion, slow queries, high memory. Put high-cardinality identifiers in the log <em>line<\/em> and filter or parse them at query time; that is what Loki's design is optimised for.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.3#turn-alert-me-when-payments-start-failing-into-a", "sec": "4.3", "q": "Turn \"alert me when payments start failing\" into a LogQL-based rule, conceptually.", "a": "<p>A metric query over logs: <code>sum(rate({namespace=\"payments\"} |= \"payment_failed\" [5m])) &gt; 0.1<\/code>, evaluated by Loki's ruler (or mirrored into Prometheus via a recording pipeline). Same alerting mechanics as 4.2; the only difference is where the series comes from.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.4#jaeger-shows-hundreds-of-one-span-traces-diagnos", "sec": "4.4", "q": "Jaeger shows hundreds of one-span traces. Diagnosis?", "a": "<p>Context propagation is broken: each service starts a new trace instead of continuing the caller's. Look for a proxy stripping <code>traceparent<\/code>, a missing or mismatched propagator (W3C vs B3), a hand-rolled HTTP client that never injects, or an async boundary nobody instrumented.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.4#head-based-versus-tail-based-sampling-when-does-", "sec": "4.4", "q": "Head-based versus tail-based sampling: when does the difference bite?", "a": "<p>When the interesting traces are rare. Head sampling at 1% will usually miss the one 5-second request that mattered; tail sampling keeps traces that contain errors or exceed a latency threshold, at the cost of buffering complete traces in the collector (and needing all spans of a trace to reach the same collector instance).<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.4#where-should-sampling-and-redaction-be-configure", "sec": "4.4", "q": "Where should sampling and redaction be configured, and why there?", "a": "<p>In the collector. It is the platform's control point: one config change applies to every service, whereas SDK-side changes need forty teams to redeploy. This is the same \"interface owned by the platform\" argument as section 3.1, applied to telemetry.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.4#you-annotated-a-deployment-for-auto-instrumentat", "sec": "4.4", "q": "You annotated a Deployment for auto-instrumentation and nothing changed. Two most likely reasons?", "a": "<p>The annotation is on the Deployment's own metadata rather than <code>spec.template.metadata<\/code>, or the pods have not restarted since (injection happens at admission, at pod creation). Third candidate: the annotation value's <code>&lt;namespace&gt;/&lt;name&gt;<\/code> does not match an existing Instrumentation CR.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.4#how-do-traces-metrics-and-logs-tie-together-in-p", "sec": "4.4", "q": "How do traces, metrics and logs tie together in practice?", "a": "<p>Exemplars link metric buckets to sample trace IDs; logs carry the trace ID as a field so you can jump from a slow span to its exact log lines; and the collector can emit all three from the same pipeline with the same resource attributes (namespace, pod, service). \"Correlated by trace ID and resource attributes\" is the sentence.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.5#name-the-dora-four-and-the-system-that-holds-eac", "sec": "4.5", "q": "Name the DORA four and the system that holds each one's raw data in this lab.", "a": "<p>Deployment frequency: Argo CD sync counters. Lead time: git commit timestamps joined with sync timestamps. Change failure rate: sync phases plus rollout outcomes. Time to restore: alerting data (firing to resolved), or incident records. None of them live in one place, which is itself the point.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.5#why-increase-rather-than-the-raw-counter-for-dep", "sec": "4.5", "q": "Why <code>increase()<\/code> rather than the raw counter for deployment frequency?", "a": "<p>The raw counter is cumulative since the controller started and resets on restart. <code>increase(...[24h])<\/code> gives the count within the window and handles resets. For a daily figure that is the right shape; <code>rate<\/code> would give you a per-second number nobody wants to read.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.5#your-new-servicemonitor-for-argo-cd-produces-no-", "sec": "4.5", "q": "Your new ServiceMonitor for Argo CD produces no target. First two checks?", "a": "<p>The endpoint's <code>port<\/code> against the Service's actual port <em>name<\/em> (<code>http-metrics<\/code>, not <code>metrics<\/code>, not 8082), and the label selector against the Service's real labels. Then, as always, Prometheus's own <code>serviceMonitorSelector<\/code> and namespace selector.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.5#how-would-you-measure-request-to-fulfillment-for", "sec": "4.5", "q": "How would you measure \"request to fulfillment\" for a self-service API?", "a": "<p>Difference between the CR's <code>metadata.creationTimestamp<\/code> and the <code>lastTransitionTime<\/code> of its Ready condition. Aggregate it by exporting that delta as a metric (a small exporter or a recording rule over kube-state-metrics custom resource state), then track its p50/p95 over time.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.5#someone-proposes-measuring-developer-productivit", "sec": "4.5", "q": "Someone proposes measuring developer productivity by lines of code. Reply in one sentence.", "a": "<p>Measure outcomes, not output: DORA's four keys plus platform-effectiveness measures (fulfillment latency, adoption, satisfaction) describe whether the system delivers value, whereas line counts reward volume and are trivially gamed.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.6#every-pod-in-a-namespace-is-running-and-the-app-", "sec": "4.6", "q": "Every pod in a namespace is Running and the app returns nothing. First three moves?", "a": "<p>Make something try (a curl or nslookup from inside the namespace) so there is evidence to read; check endpoints/readiness for the Service; check policy verdicts (Hubble DROPPED) and DNS. \"Running\" says the kubelet is happy, nothing more.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.6#a-pod-is-crashloopbackoff-and-kubectl-logs-shows", "sec": "4.6", "q": "A pod is CrashLoopBackOff and <code>kubectl logs<\/code> shows nothing useful.", "a": "<p><code>kubectl logs --previous<\/code>: you are reading the current, not-yet-started container. Then <code>describe<\/code> for the exit code and reason (137 with OOMKilled points at memory, 1 with app output points at config), then the previous container's <code>lastState.terminated<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.6#nothing-can-be-created-cluster-wide-every-apply-", "sec": "4.6", "q": "Nothing can be created cluster-wide; every apply times out with a webhook error. What happened and what is the lever?", "a": "<p>An admission webhook's backend is down and its <code>failurePolicy<\/code> is <code>Fail<\/code>, so the API server refuses writes it cannot validate. Restore the backend, or (as a deliberate emergency step) remove/scope the webhook configuration. That is why <code>failurePolicy<\/code> is a governance decision, not a default to accept blindly (5.2).<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.6#you-have-four-minutes-left-and-no-theory-that-fi", "sec": "4.6", "q": "You have four minutes left and no theory that fits the evidence. What do you do?", "a": "<p>Go back to step 1 and widen: re-check blast radius and timeline rather than digging deeper into the current object. Most stuck diagnoses are the result of over-committing to the first plausible layer. On the exam, flag it and move on; a stuck task costs you two easy ones.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.6#what-proves-an-incident-is-over", "sec": "4.6", "q": "What proves an incident is over?", "a": "<p>The same signal that showed the failure, now succeeding: the failing curl returns 200, the alert resolves, the validation check passes. A pod showing Running proves only that a container started.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.1#define-developer-once-and-grant-it-in-twelve-nam", "sec": "5.1", "q": "Define \"developer\" once and grant it in twelve namespaces. What objects, and how many?", "a": "<p>One ClusterRole plus twelve RoleBindings (each referencing that ClusterRole, each in its namespace). Not a ClusterRoleBinding: that would grant everywhere and fail the \"and not in the other namespace\" check.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.1#a-user-can-get-a-pod-by-name-but-kubectl-get-pod", "sec": "5.1", "q": "A user can <code>get<\/code> a pod by name but <code>kubectl get pods<\/code> fails. Why?", "a": "<p><code>list<\/code> is a separate verb from <code>get<\/code>, and a rule with <code>resourceNames<\/code> cannot grant a plain <code>list<\/code>. Add <code>list<\/code> (and <code>watch<\/code> if anything streams), accepting that list exposes every object of that kind in the namespace.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.1#how-do-you-let-a-tenant-read-pod-logs-but-never-", "sec": "5.1", "q": "How do you let a tenant read pod logs but never exec into a pod?", "a": "<p>Grant <code>get<\/code> on <code>pods/log<\/code> and do not grant <code>create<\/code> on <code>pods/exec<\/code>. Subresources are distinct resource strings, which is what makes this expressible at all.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.1#you-ship-a-new-crd-how-do-existing-view-users-ge", "sec": "5.1", "q": "You ship a new CRD. How do existing <code>view<\/code> users get read access without editing the built-in role?", "a": "<p>Create a ClusterRole with read verbs on your kind and label it <code>rbac.authorization.k8s.io/aggregate-to-view: \"true\"<\/code>. Aggregation folds it into the built-in role automatically, the standard platform-team move when adding an API.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.1#sealed-secrets-versus-eso-which-would-you-pick-f", "sec": "5.1", "q": "Sealed Secrets versus ESO: which would you pick for a regulated environment with an existing vault, and why?", "a": "<p>ESO: the vault stays the single source of truth with its own audit and rotation, and git holds only references; nothing secret is ever committed, encrypted or not. Sealed Secrets suits clusters with no external store, at the cost of managing (and backing up) the sealing key yourself.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.1#is-a-kubernetes-secret-encrypted", "sec": "5.1", "q": "Is a Kubernetes Secret encrypted?", "a": "<p>Not by itself: base64 is encoding. At rest it is only encrypted if the API server is configured with an EncryptionConfiguration (optionally KMS-backed); in transit it is protected by TLS. Access control (who has <code>get secrets<\/code>) is doing most of the actual work.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.2#a-require-requests-policy-never-fires-in-a-tenan", "sec": "5.2", "q": "A \"require requests\" policy never fires in a tenant namespace. Why?", "a": "<p>Its LimitRange defaults the requests during <em>mutating<\/em> admission, before the validating webhook runs, so the object Kyverno inspects already complies. Admission order (mutate then validate) is the whole explanation, and it generalises to every defaulting mechanism.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.2#failurepolicy-fail-versus-ignore-argue-both-then", "sec": "5.2", "q": "<code>failurePolicy: Fail<\/code> versus <code>Ignore<\/code>: argue both, then say what bounds the risk.", "a": "<p>Fail: no unvalidated writes, at the cost of the API server refusing writes when the webhook is down (a cluster-wide outage from a policy pod). Ignore: writes keep flowing, at the cost of a bypass window. Bound it with a tight <code>namespaceSelector<\/code>, low <code>timeoutSeconds<\/code>, exemptions for system namespaces, and running the engine with enough replicas that it is not a single point of failure.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.2#you-tighten-a-policy-to-deny-what-happens-to-the", "sec": "5.2", "q": "You tighten a policy to Deny. What happens to the workloads that already violate it?", "a": "<p>Nothing: admission is write-time. They keep running until their next write (a rollout, a scale, a controller re-create), and then they fail, possibly at 3am. That is why the Audit → report → fix → Enforce sequence exists, and why you check the reports before flipping the dial.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.2#which-engine-would-you-pick-to-also-create-a-def", "sec": "5.2", "q": "Which engine would you pick to also <em>create<\/em> a default NetworkPolicy in every new namespace?", "a": "<p>Kyverno: generation is a first-class capability there (generate rules that create and keep resources in sync). Gatekeeper validates and can mutate but does not generate; ValidatingAdmissionPolicy only validates. Matching capability to requirement is the whole question.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.2#why-might-a-platform-team-prefer-validatingadmis", "sec": "5.2", "q": "Why might a platform team prefer ValidatingAdmissionPolicy for a simple rule?", "a": "<p>No extra controller, no webhook, no availability dependency, no version skew; the API server evaluates CEL in-process. The trade is a narrower feature set (no mutation in older versions, no generation, no reporting), so it suits simple invariants rather than a governance programme.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.3#name-the-three-profiles-in-one-clause-each", "sec": "5.3", "q": "Name the three profiles in one clause each.", "a": "<p>Privileged: no restrictions. Baseline: blocks known privilege escalations (privileged containers, host namespaces, hostPath, extra capabilities). Restricted: additionally requires non-root, no privilege escalation, all capabilities dropped, and RuntimeDefault seccomp.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.3#a-deployment-is-created-and-no-pods-appear-where", "sec": "5.3", "q": "A Deployment is created and no pods appear. Where is the PSS error?", "a": "<p>On the ReplicaSet: PSS is a pod-level admission decision, so the Deployment controller records the rejection one level down. <code>kubectl describe rs -l …<\/code> or namespace events.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.3#you-want-to-know-who-breaks-if-you-tighten-a-nam", "sec": "5.3", "q": "You want to know who breaks if you tighten a namespace. Command?", "a": "<p><code>kubectl label --dry-run=server --overwrite ns &lt;ns&gt; pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted<\/code>: the server evaluates existing pods and warns for each violator, changing nothing.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.3#why-pin-enforce-version", "sec": "5.3", "q": "Why pin <code>enforce-version<\/code>?", "a": "<p>Because profiles gain checks in new Kubernetes releases: with <code>latest<\/code>, a cluster upgrade can start rejecting pods that were compliant before. Pinning turns that into a deliberate migration you schedule rather than a surprise you discover.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.3#pss-or-kyverno-for-images-must-come-from-our-reg", "sec": "5.3", "q": "PSS or Kyverno for \"images must come from our registry\"?", "a": "<p>Kyverno (or Gatekeeper): PSS covers only the fixed pod-hardening dimension and has no notion of registries. PSS is the floor; the engine is for organisation-specific rules, and it is fine, indeed normal, to run both.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.4#which-audit-level-would-you-set-for-secrets-and-", "sec": "5.4", "q": "Which audit level would you set for Secrets, and which for Events? Why?", "a": "<p>Secrets: <code>Metadata<\/code>, never Request or RequestResponse, or you write secret values into a log file. Events: <code>None<\/code>; they are high-volume and low-value, and dropping them keeps the log readable. First-match-wins ordering makes those two rules the top of the file.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.4#find-every-request-a-specific-serviceaccount-was", "sec": "5.4", "q": "Find every request a specific ServiceAccount was denied. Shape of the query?", "a": "<p>Filter on <code>.user.username == \"system:serviceaccount:&lt;ns&gt;:&lt;name&gt;\"<\/code> and either <code>.responseStatus.code == 403<\/code> or <code>.annotations[\"authorization.k8s.io/decision\"] == \"forbid\"<\/code>, then print verb and objectRef. That is the audit-log form of the RBAC debugging you did in 5.1.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.4#an-sbom-and-a-vulnerability-report-what-is-the-d", "sec": "5.4", "q": "An SBOM and a vulnerability report: what is the difference and why keep both?", "a": "<p>The SBOM is an inventory of components; the vulnerability report is that inventory joined against a CVE database at a point in time. Keep both because the inventory stays true while the CVE list changes daily; that is exactly why continuous rescanning (the operator) matters more than a scan at build time alone.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.4#why-is-compliance-as-crds-a-platform-engineering", "sec": "5.4", "q": "Why is \"compliance as CRDs\" a platform-engineering idea rather than a Trivy feature?", "a": "<p>Because it makes compliance data a first-class API object: queryable with kubectl, protected by RBAC, watchable by controllers, graphable in Grafana, and reviewable in git. The alternative, reports as files in a bucket, cannot be joined to anything the platform already does.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.4#your-cluster-s-api-server-will-not-start-after-a", "sec": "5.4", "q": "Your cluster's API server will not start after an audit change. First hypothesis?", "a": "<p>The audit policy file is missing or unparseable at the path given by <code>--audit-policy-file<\/code> (or the volume mount is wrong). The API server refuses to start rather than run unaudited: a safety choice that presents as a dead control plane.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.5#we-installed-a-service-mesh-so-all-traffic-is-en", "sec": "5.5", "q": "\"We installed a service mesh, so all traffic is encrypted.\" Correct?", "a": "<p>No. The default is PERMISSIVE, which accepts plaintext as well as mTLS so migration can be incremental. You need a PeerAuthentication in STRICT mode (mesh-wide, per namespace, or per workload), and you should prove it by watching a non-mesh client get refused.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.5#where-does-a-workload-s-mesh-identity-come-from-", "sec": "5.5", "q": "Where does a workload's mesh identity come from, and why does that matter to section 5.1?", "a": "<p>From its ServiceAccount: <code>spiffe://cluster.local/ns/&lt;ns&gt;/sa/&lt;sa&gt;<\/code>. It matters because your RBAC design now doubles as your network authorization design (one identity, two enforcement points), and because sloppy SA reuse silently widens both.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.5#you-need-to-allow-only-post-to-orders-from-one-s", "sec": "5.5", "q": "You need to allow only POST to <code>/orders<\/code> from one service. What must exist?", "a": "<p>An AuthorizationPolicy with an L7 <code>to.operation<\/code> rule <em>and<\/em> a waypoint proxy for the target, because ztunnel alone enforces L4. Without the waypoint the method/path clauses have nothing to evaluate them.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.5#networkpolicy-or-authorizationpolicy-when-do-you", "sec": "5.5", "q": "NetworkPolicy or AuthorizationPolicy: when do you use each?", "a": "<p>Both, at different layers. NetworkPolicy is CNI-enforced, identity-by-label, works for all traffic including non-mesh workloads. AuthorizationPolicy is mesh-enforced, identity-by-certificate, survives IP churn and works across clusters. Defence in depth: the CNI keeps the blast radius small, the mesh makes the caller prove who it is.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.5#give-a-scenario-where-spire-beats-a-mesh", "sec": "5.5", "q": "Give a scenario where SPIRE beats a mesh.", "a": "<p>A workload must authenticate to something outside the cluster (a managed database, a partner API, a legacy service) with short-lived credentials and no static secret. SPIRE issues an SVID through the Workload API socket; the mesh only secures traffic between meshed services. Bonus: SPIFFE IDs federate across trust domains, which is how you extend identity beyond one cluster.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.6#why-scan-in-the-pipeline-and-in-the-cluster", "sec": "5.6", "q": "Why scan in the pipeline <em>and<\/em> in the cluster?", "a": "<p>The pipeline gate blocks known-bad before publication; it can say nothing about CVEs disclosed after the build. Continuous in-cluster scanning catches those, on images already running. Only-CI leaves you blind to every CVE younger than your last deploy; only-cluster lets bad images ship in the first place.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.6#why-is-signing-by-digest-not-by-tag-the-point", "sec": "5.6", "q": "Why is signing by digest, not by tag, the point?", "a": "<p>A digest names content; a tag is a mutable pointer. Signing the digest means the signature covers exactly those bytes, and anyone who moves the tag cannot forge it. Verification resolves the tag to a digest before checking, and a good admission policy also rewrites the pod's image to that digest so the runtime cannot drift from what you verified.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.6#signatures-exist-and-someone-still-deployed-an-u", "sec": "5.6", "q": "Signatures exist and someone still deployed an unsigned image. What was missing?", "a": "<p>Admission verification. Signing without an enforcement point is decoration: nothing checks it at the door. Add an image-verification policy that matches the registry glob and denies on failure, and make sure it matches the reference form the pods actually use.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.6#kyverno-reports-no-signatures-found-for-an-image", "sec": "5.6", "q": "Kyverno reports \"no signatures found\" for an image you definitely signed. Three candidates?", "a": "<p>Registry name/reachability (Kyverno resolves from inside the cluster, so <code>localhost<\/code> is wrong), scheme (plain http needs the engine's insecure flag), and signature <em>format<\/em> skew (a new-bundle signature where the verifier expects the legacy <code>.sig<\/code> tag). Also check the transparency-log expectation if you signed without uploading to Rekor.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.6#distinguish-sbom-provenance-and-signature-in-one", "sec": "5.6", "q": "Distinguish SBOM, provenance and signature in one sentence each.", "a": "<p>SBOM: what is inside the artifact. Provenance: how and by whom it was built (a SLSA-style attestation). Signature: who vouches for these exact bytes. Three documents, one trust story; provenance and SBOMs are themselves usually signed attestations.<\/p>"}
];
